#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Record everything an Interaxon Muse headband streams over Bluetooth LE
(EEG electrodes, accelerometer, gyroscope, PPG, battery/telemetry) and
stream it as CSV on stdout (or to a file), in a format ``live-viewer`` can
plot live (see README.md).

The script talks to the headband directly over BLE (via ``bleak`` on top of
BlueZ); no vendor app or SDK is involved.

Output contract
----------------
stdout carries ONLY clean CSV (one header row, then one data row per
sample). All logging/diagnostics go to stderr. Rows from all sensors are
merged into a single stream whose ``timestamp_ns`` column is globally
non-decreasing (required by ``live-viewer``). Each row belongs to exactly
one ``stream`` and fills only that stream's columns; the others are empty.
Missing samples are left empty -- NEVER interpolated/guessed.

Columns::

    timestamp_ns, timestamp_iso, stream,
    [seq]                                  (only with --include-seq)
    TP9, AF7, AF8, TP10, AUX,              (stream "eeg",    uV,    256 Hz)
    drl, ref,                              (stream "drlref", uV,  ~28.4 Hz)
    ax, ay, az,                            (stream "acc",    g,    ~51 Hz)
    gx, gy, gz,                            (stream "gyro",   deg/s, ~51 Hz)
    ppg_ambient, ppg_ir, ppg_red,          (stream "ppg",    raw,    64 Hz)
    battery_pct, fuel_gauge_mv, adc_mv, temperature_raw
                                           (stream "telemetry", ~0.1 Hz)
    [raw_hex]                              (only with --include-raw-bytes)

``seq`` is the headband's own 16-bit packet counter of the BLE packet the
sample came from (a jump by more than 1 within a stream = lost packets).

Timestamps
----------
``timestamp_ns`` is the estimated *sampling* time of each individual
sample, in ns since the Unix epoch: every packet carries a sequence number,
so a sample's position on the headband's own sample clock is known exactly
(including across lost packets); that clock's rate is measured and it is
anchored to the host's wall clock (``time.time_ns()`` at BLE packet
arrival) by tracking the least delayed packets (see SampleClock). Rows are
held back for ``--reorder-latency`` so they can be emitted in time order
across sensors. Telemetry rows simply carry their packet's arrival time.

Lab Streaming Layer
-------------------
With ``--lsl`` the rows go to one LSL outlet (``--lsl-name``, default
``Muse``) instead of CSV, the same way ``epoc-direct-record --lsl`` publishes
an EPOC X: one float32 sample per CSV row, the channels being the CSV columns
without the two timestamps (carried by the sample's LSL timestamp, in unix
seconds) and with ``stream`` as a number (index into the desc's
``stream_codes``, e.g. 0 = eeg); empty cells are NaN. The desc also holds
``manufacturer``, ``device_name``, ``device_address`` and per-channel
label/type/unit. ``epoc-direct-record/save-lsl.py`` saves such a stream back
to muse-record's CSV.

Example usage::

    ./muse-record | live-viewer --width=5s --group=eeg --group=acc ...
    ./muse-record --lsl --lsl-name 'Muse'

See README.md for setup and the full live-viewer invocation.
"""

